    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d432">

                <pb facs="0692" n="578" xml:id="pg578"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">654.0031/10: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-01T14:00:00+01:00">April 1, 1931—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:25 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>43. Department’s 38, March 30, 5 p.m.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d432-f1"/> When the
                    customs union between Liechtenstein and Switzerland was set up, the Swiss
                    Government was a party to several treaties which contained the
                    most-favored-nation clause. Several had clauses which, in the case of the
                    customs union, specifically excepted the most-favored-nation clause. All the
                    agreements which the Swiss have negotiated since that date have included a
                    similar proviso.</p>
                <p>No Government has ever protested against this arrangement. Dinichert<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d432-f2"/> gave me this information informally. Shall we proceed to
                    study such treaties in greater detail?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
